{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1347519-can-you-give-me-examples-of-nephandi-magic-via-the-qliphothic-spheres","id":1347519,"title":"Can you give me examples of Nephandi magic via the Qliphothic spheres?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-11-10T11:29:35-08:00","id":1347519,"title":"Can you give me examples of Nephandi magic via the Qliphothic spheres?","content":"I've played Mage a few times, but never seen any Nephandi used, and I haven't got any of the Nephandi books currently.<br /> <br /> I'd like to use some Nephandi as a group of minor antagonists (I mean, they're Mages, so not minor-minor, but I mean not as the main antagonists of the story), but I don't really understand what their magic looks like.<br /> <br /> I know it's twisted and inverted, but what does that mean in practice? What will the PCs actually see?<br /> I know it alludes to the Qliphoth (which I know a bit about as in their role in Kabbalah, not Mage's interpretation of).<br /> <br /> My friend told me its kind of weird, kind of... Cthulu-esque? Weird gribbly tentacles? Cancerous growths? That sounds pretty cool, but I'd like to know more.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-10T11:30:06-08:00","id":1347520,"title":"","content":"Are there some Nephandi rotes written up somewhere?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>STing Bronze Age Exalted","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2402-the-wizard-of-oz","name":"The Wizard of Oz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2402_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-10T15:25:39-08:00","id":1347563,"title":"","content":"There are Nephandic rotes - in the yet to be fully released <i>Book Of The Fallen</i>. Backers have it for errata, but it will still be a short while before it hits the online shelves. It's all about Nephandi, front to back.<br /> <br /> The rotes in the book are very much about<br /> <br /> * Hiding their true nature<br /> * Spotting flaws and guilt in people, seeing how they see themselves and prying at personal secrets<br /> * Seducing them<br /> * Amplyfying their urges<br /> * Riling them up - indviduals or whole groups<br /> * Turning truths into falsehoods or falsehoods into truth in the minds of people, and fillign them with convictions they should not have<br /> * Torture<br /> * Letting all hell break loose if necessary and<br /> * Vanishing<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-11T11:50:02-08:00","id":1347726,"title":"","content":"Been a while since I read the section, but the <i>Book of Madness</i> explains it as Qliphothic Spheres being metaphysically destructive. As in, regardless of Paradigm, Reality is made tangibly damaged by a Nephandi simply doing magick at all.<br /> <br /> Qliphothic Correspondence, for instance, doesn't bridge two locations when the mage teleports. It erases the space in between (or perhaps gouges out a passage is a better description). Qliphothic Mind doesn't rearrange or suppress memories, it annihilates them.<br /> <br /> Functionally, Nephandi cast spells the same as any other. On a metaphysical level, a Fallen's magick is a blight on Reality itself.<br /> <br /> <br /> As for Focus (Paradigm, Practice, Instruments), Nephandi &quot;do magick&quot; in as varied ways as regular Mages. Many Nephandi WERE regular Mages until they fell (called Barabbi), and carried their Practices and Instruments with them. The difference is that where a regular mage's methods might call for occasional grisly or unseemly works - a sacrifice here, a scarification there, etc. - Nephandi almost always modify existing praxis towards the most depraved and cruel ends. Purposefully vile, as the evil isn't just a means to an end, but an end unto itself.<br /> <br /> Instead of the solemn and holy prayers of the Chorister, you get a black mass exhorting Satan to drown the world in blood and fire. Instead of a Hermetic ritual circle inscribed with celestial names and sacred mathematics, it is an inverted design created according to black geometry. Instead of an animal sacrifice that is swift and respectful, cruel and bloody human sacrifice becomes standard operating procedure. Instead of the elegant automatons of a utopian scientist, it's the brutal and inhumane machinery that would feel right at home in <a href=\"https://mtg.gamepedia.com/Phyrexia\" target=\"_blank\">Phyrexia</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokyo_Gore_Police\" target=\"_blank\">Tokyo Gore Police</a>, or H.R. Geiger's dreams.<br /> <br /> Basically, take the magickal styles practiced by other Mages. Now imagine the most extreme, least wholesome ways to go about these workings. Then bend that praxis to the most inhumane, cruel, destructive, and corruptive uses possible. To use a line from Zefrank, THAT is how the Nephandus do.<br /> <br /> <br /> Also yes, some Nephandi channel Lovecraft and the unwholesome cults portrayed in his works. An entire faction of Nephandi, in fact; the K'llashaa. Heirs to ancient and precise methods passed down through families, through cult initiations, and forbidden tomes, the K'llashaa perform grisly rites pioneered in the ancient days to glorify the Great Ones and Elder Evils that dwell beyond the furthest stars (and beyond the Horizon, in the Deep Umbra). Inflicting suffering and shedding blood, in order to funnel to their eldritch masters. Laboring tirelessly and methodically on plots to open the doors - when the stars are just right - and usher their masters into the mortal world once again.<br /> <br /> Even among other Nephandi - like the Infernalists (traditional devil worshipers) and Malfeans (adherents to abstract qualities of evil, as embodied by fragments of the Wyrm) - the K'llashaa are considered thoroughly mad and disquieting. Quite an achievement, if it could be called such.<br /> <br /> Disappointingly, the Book of Madness insists that K'llashaa don't go for the traditional Lovecraft staples - statues of unspeakable horrors or alien geometries - but rather employ practical tools for inflicting pain and shedding blood. Probably a nod to the fact that K'llashaa especially tend to get hunted down most regularly of Nephandic cells, because they are the least subtle. So only the most subtle of this group is liable to survive for long. Personally, I LIKE the image of cultists chanting before a stone statue of Cthulhu or the like, and would prefer to have it show up more here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-11T15:18:01-08:00","id":1347775,"title":"","content":"I'm in agreement on that last. There's room among the Outsider faction for the occasional measured, calculating sorcerer like <span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial,Sans-Serif\">Ephraim Waite, but by and large they should be the gibbering lunatics and degenerate cultists dancing around creepy idols in the wastelands.</span></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-11-11T22:24:44-08:00","id":1347836,"title":"","content":"While we're on the subject, there's a notable difference of attitude between Revised and M20, when it comes to Mages who play into the pageantry and tropes of the archetypes they follow. In Revised, the assumption was that every Mage was being as low-key as possible, and that the 90s-00s era Mages were too cool to &quot;look the part&quot;. Whereas M20 enshrines playing into the traits classically associated with their archetypes, by emphasizing the mechanical advantages to adhering to &quot;Mythic Threads&quot;. Any Hermetic Wizard could go around in a leather jacket and discreet wand/ring, but they could gain actual <i>reductions</i> on casting difficulty (or even make certain Effects Coincidental, depending on context) by dressing up in a pointy hat and robes and brandishing an ornate staff.<br /> <br /> It's as if to say, &quot;Yes, there are inarguable advantages to being discreet, but there are also advantages to playing to type&quot;.<br /> <br /> Naturally, Book of Madness was a Revised era book, and was therefore of the opinion that a Mage - especially a Nephandus - dressing and acting according to their archetype was dumb and only done by the suicidal. In M20, though, a K'llashaa cultist has an incentive to ACT like the gibbering lunatic who holds degenerate rites before a basalt statue of Nyarlathotep. Sure, it's riskier than doing the Ephraim Waite thing, but there are <i>benefits</i> to that risk.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}